\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The membrane equation}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let #{V} be the voltage across a membrane that is the same at every
point, so #{V} is one function of time.
\citet{§1.5, p. 11}{ermentrout2010mathematical} calls such a membrane
isopotential. For each ionic current, indexed by #{k}, the
\newvocab{reversal potential} #{E_{k}} is the voltage at which that
current vanishes. The current is then #{g_{k}(V-E_{k})}, where
#{g_{k}} is its conductance. When #{g_{k}} does not depend on time
or on #{V}, the current is Ohmic.}

\p{The \newvocab{membrane equation} is the current balance
##{C\dot V=I_{\mathrm{app}}-\sum_{k} g_{k}(V-E_{k}).}
#{C} is the capacitance and #{I_{\mathrm{app}}} is the applied
current. The applied current is positive when it enters the cell,
and each ionic current #{g_{k}(V-E_{k})} is positive when positive
charge leaves the cell. This is the balance in
\citet{(1.12), §1.4, p. 10}{ermentrout2010mathematical}, where the
applied term is #{I(t)/A}, and in
\citet{(2.3), §2.1.3, p. 28}{izhikevich2007dynamical}.
\citet{(5.5) and (5.6), §5.3, draft pp. 9–10}{dayan2001theoretical}
writes it as #{c_{m}\dot V=-i_{m}+I_{e}/A} with
#{i_{m}=\sum_{i} g_{i}(V-E_{i})}.
\citet{pp. 105–107}{nicholls2012neuron} writes the sodium and
potassium currents in the same form,
#{i_{\mathrm{Na}}=g_{\mathrm{Na}}(V_{m}-E_{\mathrm{Na}})} and
#{i_{\mathrm{K}}=g_{\mathrm{K}}(V_{m}-E_{\mathrm{K}})}.}

\p{When one ion carries the current, #{E_{k}} is that ion's Nernst
potential: the voltage at which the electrical force and the force
from the concentration difference are equal and opposite.
\citet{§1.2, p. 3}{ermentrout2010mathematical} states that balance.}

